  "summary": "Preprints pair mouse single-nucleus atlases with human brain and CSF proteomics to resolve Alzheimer's disease into cell-type-specific and molecular subtypes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is high-dimensional omics used to decompose Alzheimer's heterogeneity. In 5xFAD mice, snRNA-seq identifies VIP interneurons as most disrupted in synapse programs and preferentially rescued by rolipram, implicating a Pde7b/CREB1 cAMP axis. A harmonized resource of 1,863 mouse cortex and hippocampus transcriptomes, including AD models, supports cross-study comparison. In humans, peptide-centric proteomics of comorbidity-free brains yields two sporadic AD subtypes distinct from autosomal dominant AD, while diffusion geometry and persistent homology of ADNI CSF proteomics find two proteomic states differing in tau biomarkers. Whether the brain and CSF stratifications correspond is not addressed in these items. A plasma proteomic atlas of neurodevelopmental disorders reports neurodegenerative overlap, and the Parkinson gut biomarker study is peripheral to this thread.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
